                                        "transcript":  "So first I needed a first case. Something to test whether the room actually works.\n\nYou have this patient: Yaw Boateng, 22 years old, no medical history. She walks into the ER with abdominal pain, vomiting for two days, and she cannot stay awake. The point-of-care glucose reads north of 600. Her pancreas has stopped making insulin.\n\nIn DKA, the body turns to fat for fuel because glucose is there but can\u0027t enter the cells. The body is clever—it always has a backup. So it breaks down fat into ketones. Ketones are an alternate fuel, but they don\u0027t burn clean. They make the blood acidic. That acid consumes all the bicarbonate, the buffer system. As it keeps sucking it up, the breathing gets deep and fast. The mucous membranes go dry from dehydration and the respiratory muscle activation. And the abdominal pain—classic DKA presentation—mimics an acute surgical abdomen so well that inexperienced clinicians order CT scans instead of insulin.\n\nI set up this case like a timed room: glucose is high, the anion gap is wide, potassium is borderline, and the clock is ticking. \n\nThe video below is a visual metaphor—a pancreas that should be pumping out insulin but stays silent. You prod it, you wait, nothing happens. In DKA, the organ is there, but it’s not responding. This is the first case in the room—real physiology, real consequences, no textbook chapter. By running through it again and again, you start to recognize the pattern instinctively through sheer practice.",
